    detailedAnswer: "Source / report context:\n[REPORTED] Implement LRU cache on paper. Reported by: OLX Groups. Source: GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview Experience. ==================================================== 3. ARRAYS ====================================================\n\nSource classification: [REPORTED]\n\nAn LRU cache needs O(1) average-time lookup, insertion, and\neviction. The standard design combines a hash map from key to a node\nwith a doubly linked list ordered from most recently used to least\nrecently used. get() moves a found node to the front; put() updates an\nexisting node or inserts a new one at the front, and when capacity is\nexceeded removes the tail. Hash-map lookup and constant-time list\nunlink/insert give O(1) average time.\n\nExample:\nC++ design: class LRUCache { int cap; list<pair<int,int>> dq;\n// front = most recently used unordered_map<int,\nlist<pair<int,int>>::iterator> pos; public: int get(int key) { auto it =\npos.find(key); if (it == pos.end()) return -1; int value =\nit->second->second; dq.splice(dq.begin(), dq, it->second); return value;\n}\n\n    void put(int key, int value) {\n        if (auto it = pos.find(key); it != pos.end()) {\n            it->second->second = value;\n            dq.splice(dq.begin(), dq, it->second);\n            return;\n        }\n        dq.push_front({key, value});\n        pos[key] = dq.begin();\n        if ((int)dq.size() > cap) {\n            auto last = prev(dq.end());\n            pos.erase(last->first);\n            dq.pop_back();\n        }\n    }\n\n};\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart by restating the problem, confirm constraints,\ndiscuss a straightforward approach, then optimize. Explain the invariant\nthat makes the solution correct.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nProblem decomposition, data-structure\nselection, correctness reasoning, complexity analysis, edge-case\nhandling, and the ability to communicate while coding.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n• Hash map + doubly linked list is the standard\nO(1)-average design. • Keep map and list state consistent. • Define\neviction and update semantics explicitly.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n• Can you improve the time or space\ncomplexity? • What changes if the input is very large? • What edge cases\nwould you test? • Can you implement it iteratively instead of\nrecursively (or vice versa)?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n• Coding before clarifying the problem statement. •\nGiving an incorrect Big-O claim. • Ignoring duplicates, empty input, or\nboundary conditions. • Using a data structure without explaining why it\nprovides the required complexity.",
    keyPoints: "• Hash map + doubly linked list is the standard\nO(1)-average design. • Keep map and list state consistent. • Define\neviction and update semantics explicitly.",
